    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Strong BTC fit. Low floor (subtract the fee, divide by the rate). The 15-minute block is a productive stumble: most groups will start by treating x as hours and have to re-examine units. Q2 (break-even comparison) and Q4 (interpretation: 'could it have been under 30 minutes?') push reasoning beyond plug-and-chug.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Many groups start by writing 42 + 9x = 123, then solve x = 9. The interpretation question is: 9 what? Strong groups label x as '15-minute blocks' and convert: 9 blocks = 135 minutes = 2 hr 15 min. Start 6:20, end 8:35.\n- Some groups try 42 + 9h = 123 where h is hours. They get h ≈ 9, which makes them suspicious — no locksmith works 9 hours on one lock. This is the productive discovery.\n- For Q2, set 42 + 9x = 95 → 9x = 53 → x ≈ 5.89 blocks ≈ 88 minutes. Quick-Click is cheaper than Bolt Brothers' $95 only if the job finishes in under ~88 minutes. Marisol's took 135 min, so the neighbor was right.\n- For Q3, the equation becomes 42 + 9(x − 1) = T, or equivalently 33 + 9x = T with x ≥ 1. Marisol's bill drops by $9 to $114.\n- For Q4, 42 + 9x = 69 → x = 3 blocks = 45 minutes. So 'under 30 minutes' is impossible at this bill — would require x < 2.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Treating the $9 as 'per hour' instead of 'per 15 minutes.' The arithmetic still gives x = 9, but the interpretation is wildly wrong.\n- Forgetting to subtract the call-out fee before dividing (123 ÷ 9 ≈ 13.7, meaningless).\n- For Q2, comparing only Marisol's actual bill ($123 vs $95) and concluding Bolt is always cheaper — missing that the answer depends on job length.\n- For Q3, subtracting $9 from the final bill but forgetting to also subtract one block of time, or vice versa.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- What does the '9' in your equation represent? What are its units? How did you check?\n- For Q2, is Bolt Brothers ALWAYS the better deal? When does Quick-Click win?\n- In Q3, the call-out fee didn't change, but the equation did. What part of the model captures 'the first 15 minutes are free'?\n- The cousin's bill in Q4 was less than Marisol's. Could it ever be possible to get a Quick-Click bill of exactly $42? Of less than $42?",
    "extensions": "- Graph total bill vs. number of 15-minute blocks for both companies on the same axes. Where do the lines cross? What does the crossing point mean? What does the y-intercept of each line mean?\n- Quick-Click is considering a new pricing model: $30 call-out fee plus $12 per 15 minutes. For what job lengths would the new model be cheaper than the old one for the customer? For what job lengths would Quick-Click make more money?\n- Write your OWN two-step equation word problem with a context that isn't service-call-billing. Trade with another group and solve.",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Let x = number of 15-minute blocks. Equation: 42 + 9x = 123. Solve: 9x = 81, x = 9 blocks = 9 × 15 = 135 minutes = 2 hr 15 min. Marisol drove away at 6:20 + 2:15 = 8:35 p.m.\n2. Bolt Brothers ($95) vs Quick-Click: 42 + 9x = 95 → x = 53/9 ≈ 5.89 blocks ≈ 88 minutes. If the job takes longer than ~88 minutes, Bolt is cheaper. Marisol's took 135 minutes, so YES — the neighbor was right; she would have saved $123 − $95 = $28.\n3. New equation: 42 + 9(x − 1) = T (with x ≥ 1), which simplifies to 33 + 9x = T. With x = 9 (same 9 blocks of work, first one free): T = 33 + 81 = $114. Marisol saves exactly $9.\n4. 42 + 9x = 69 → 9x = 27 → x = 3 blocks = 45 minutes. The job could not have been done in under 30 minutes because that would require x < 2 blocks, giving a bill of at most 42 + 18 = $60 < $69.",
